Yes, both are valid and do exactly the same thing.

javascript
const {id} = req.params
is just destructuring — it pulls the id property off the req.params object and puts it in a variable called id. It's shorthand for:

javascript
const id = req.params.id
So this:

javascript
const UpDateQuestion = async(req,res)=>{
    const {id} = req.params
    // id === req.params.id, same value
}
They're interchangeable — {id} = req.params is just the more common/idiomatic style in Express handlers, especially once you're using id more than once in the function (cleaner than typing req.params.id repeatedly).

The bug I flagged earlier in GetById was different — there you had:

javascript
const {question, anwer} = req.params.id
That destructures from req.params.id, which is a string (like "64abc123..."), not an object. Destructuring a string for properties like question/answer just gives undefined for both, since strings don't have those keys.

So the fix is either:

javascript
const {id} = req.params        // ✅ destructure from req.params
or

javascript
const id = req.params.id       // ✅ same thing, no destructuring
Not:

javascript
const {question, anwer} = req.params.id   // ❌ wrong source object
