Good question — let's fix the bugs first, then talk about PATCH.

Bugs in this function
1. findByIdAndUpdate({id}) is wrong

javascript
const updateQuestion = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate({id});
You're passing an object {id: "64abc..."} as the id argument, when Mongoose expects the raw id string as the first argument. findByIdAndUpdate actually takes three arguments:

javascript
Question.findByIdAndUpdate(id, updateData, options)
Right now you're not passing any update data at all — so even if the id worked, nothing would actually get updated. You need the new values from req.body:

javascript
const updateQuestion = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(
    id,
    req.body,
    { new: true, runValidators: true }
);
id — the raw string, not wrapped in {}
req.body — the fields to update, e.g. { question: "...", answer: "...", difficulty: "..." }
{ new: true } — without this, Mongoose returns the old document (pre-update), which is confusing when you send it back as "updated" data
runValidators: true — makes your schema validation actually run on update (Mongoose skips it by default on updates)
2. updateQuestion === 0 will never be true

javascript
if(!updateQuestion || updateQuestion === 0){
findByIdAndUpdate resolves to either a document or null — never 0. That check is dead code, harmless but pointless. Just:

javascript
if(!updateQuestion){
    return res.status(404).json({ message:'this id or question does not exist' })
}
(also add the 404 status — right now this falls back to 200)

Fixed version
javascript
const UpDateQuestion = async(req,res)=>{
    const {id} = req.params;
    try{
        const updateQuestion = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(
            id,
            req.body,
            { new: true, runValidators: true }
        );
        if(!updateQuestion){
            return res.status(404).json({
                message:'this id or question does not exist'
            })
        }
        return res.status(200).json({
            message:'Question updated Successfully',
            data: updateQuestion
        })
    } catch(error){
        return res.status(500).json({
            message:'something went wrong',
            data: error.message
        })
    }
}
PATCH vs PUT — is it "the same method" here?
The HTTP method (PATCH/PUT) isn't set in the controller function at all — it's set in your route definition, e.g.:

javascript
router.patch('/questions/:id', UpDateQuestion)
// or
router.put('/questions/:id', UpDateQuestion)
The controller code itself would look identical either way. But semantically they mean different things:

PATCH — partial update. Client sends only the fields they want to change, e.g. { difficulty: "hard" }. This matches your findByIdAndUpdate(id, req.body) pattern well, since Mongoose only overwrites the keys present in req.body and leaves the rest untouched.
PUT — full replacement. Client is expected to send the entire resource, and any field left out is supposed to be treated as removed/reset.
Given your function only touches whatever's in req.body and doesn't require the full object, PATCH is the more correct semantic choice here:

javascript
router.patch('/questions/:id', UpDateQuestion)
