Exactly — you've got it. The controller code (UpDateQuestion function body) stays 100% identical either way. The only place PATCH vs PUT shows up is in the route definition:

javascript
router.patch('/questions/:id', UpDateQuestion)   // partial update
router.put('/questions/:id', UpDateQuestion)     // full replace
Same function, same logic, same findByIdAndUpdate(id, req.body, {...}) call — the HTTP method is just a label/convention for how the client is expected to send data, it doesn't change how Express or Mongoose behaves. Express will run the exact same handler regardless of which verb you wired it to.

The difference only really matters if you choose to enforce it — e.g. some APIs will validate that a PUT request body contains every required field (full replacement) and reject it otherwise, while a PATCH is allowed to send just one field. But nothing in your current code makes that distinction — it happily does a partial update no matter which verb calls it. So practically, yes: same code, verb is just metadata/routing.